# Hesperus MQTT ACL
#
# Two identities with deliberately different power:
#
#   hesperus-board     — the STM32 firmware. Full read/write on its own
#                        topics: it must publish status/telemetry AND
#                        subscribe to commands.
#
#   hesperus-dashboard — the browser client (dashboard/app/hooks/useMQTT.ts).
#                        READ ONLY. Its credentials are compiled into the
#                        public JS bundle and are therefore effectively
#                        public — anyone can read them out of devtools. That
#                        is acceptable ONLY because this ACL forbids it from
#                        publishing: the worst an attacker can do with them
#                        is watch telemetry, not issue board commands.
#                        Without this ACL, handing the browser working
#                        credentials would re-open exactly the hole the
#                        2026-08-18 MQTT auth work closed (anonymous clients
#                        publishing erase_wifi/set_wifi/firmware_update).
#
# The FastAPI server connects as hesperus-board too (it must publish
# commands) — see server/app/mqtt/client.py.

user hesperus-board
topic readwrite device/stm32/#
# $SYS read is required by the container healthcheck, which does
# `mosquitto_sub -t '$SYS/#' -C 1 -W 3` (docker-compose.prod.yml). Without
# this the subscribe is silently denied, no message ever arrives, the 3s
# wait times out, and the container is marked unhealthy forever — which in
# turn blocks `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` and fails deploys.
# $SYS is broker metadata (uptime, client counts), not device data.
topic read $SYS/#

user hesperus-dashboard
topic read device/stm32/#
